  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Spring Fieldcap (Agrocybe praecox) is a small, tan to brown mushroom that fruits early in the spring, often appearing before other fungi. It belongs to the Hymenogastraceae family and is commonly found in disturbed soil and grassy areas throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap Color and Texture",
      "description": "Tan to yellowish-brown cap that is smooth and often hygrophanous, becoming paler as it dries",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills and Spore Print",
      "description": "Pale gills that darken with age, and a dark brown spore print that distinguishes it from lookalikes",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Fruiting Season",
      "description": "Appears very early in spring (March-May), often one of the first mushrooms to emerge, which is a key identifying feature",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Spring Fieldcap prefers disturbed soils, lawns, gardens, and grassy meadows throughout New England, often fruiting in clusters.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Its species name 'praecox' means 'early' in Latin, referring to its remarkable habit of fruiting in early spring when few other fungi are active.",
